# SRG-APP-000516-AU-000340  -  CAT II
# The Central Log Server that aggregates log records from hosts and devices
# must be configured to use TCP for transmission.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations. Edit this
# file to change how this requirement is assessed here; it is read at run
# time. Regenerating overwrites it.

id:              SRG-APP-000516-AU-000340
group:           V-206513
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
cci:             CCI-000366
control_rev5:    CM-6

contribution:    Splunk chooses which transports it will accept log records on, and it can
                 be configured with no connectionless receiver, leaving only its
                 acknowledged TCP receivers in service.

governs:         inputs.conf - Configures inputs, distributed inputs such as forwarders, and file system
                 monitoring.

check:           inputs.conf [udp:<port>] disabled absent
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          inputs.conf [udp:<port>] disabled https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.2-configuration-file-reference/inputs.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        The transport each sending device actually uses, carried by that device's
                 own configuration - on a Splunk forwarder, its outputs.conf
                 [tcpout:<target_group>] (outputs.conf.spec:121) - since a receiver can
                 refuse connectionless traffic but cannot make a sender switch.
external:        Network reachability of the TCP receiving port, carried by the firewall
                 and the network.

citation:        SPLK-CL-000270 check and fix text; Central Log Server SRG
                 SRG-APP-000516-AU-000340 check text; inputs.conf.spec:1314 ([udp:<port>]),
                 inputs.conf.spec:1227 ([udp://<remote server>:<port>]),
                 outputs.conf.spec:121 ([tcpout:<target_group>])
finding:         A UDP input remains configured on the aggregating instance, so the
                 application still accepts log records over a transport that does not
                 acknowledge delivery.
